<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<section id="A1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Proof of Proposition 1</h3>

<div id="A1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before we proceed with the proof, we first restate the relevant definition and proposition here for convenience. We also provide an illustrative example of anonymous walks in <a href="#A1.F6" title="In Proof of Proposition 1 ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis ‣ MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> to facilitate understanding.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09337v1/Anonymous_Walk_Example_final.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/430;" width="698" height="430"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Anonymous walks examples with the starting node colored in grey. Random walks (a) and (b) share the same anonymous walk type, even though the node sets are different. The same node collections may refer to different anonymous walk types as walk (a) and (c).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anonymous Walk</span>)<span id="Thmdefinition5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a random walk <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=(v_{0},v_{1},\ldots,v_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=(v_{0},v_{1},\ldots,v_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> on a graph, the corresponding anonymous walk is defined as a sequence of integers <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=(\gamma_{0},\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=(\gamma_{0},\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}=\min\mathrm{pos}(w,v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>pos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}=\min\mathrm{pos}(w,v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pos}(w,v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>pos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pos}(w,v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> returns the position at which the node <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> first appears in the walk <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the mapping of a random walk <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to the anonymous walk <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\mapsto a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\mapsto a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. A sufficiently rich distribution <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> over length-<math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> anonymous walks starting at <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{E}^{\prime}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{E}^{\prime}|)</annotation></semantics></math>, is sufficient to reconstruct the ego graph <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(v,h)=(\mathcal{V}^{\prime},\mathcal{E}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(v,h)=(\mathcal{V}^{\prime},\mathcal{E}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with radius <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Refer to the proof of Theorem 1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Micali and Zhu 2016</a>)</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Proof of Proposition 2</h3>

<div id="Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{H_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{H_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sufficiently set of hidden graphs, and let <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive-definite random walk kernel that distinguishes all non-isomorphic subgraphs of bounded size. If <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, then the representation <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> computed by MoSE is injective over the set of <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., MoSE simulates one round of SWL node refinement.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive-definite and <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently rich, for any pair of non-isomorphic subgraphs <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\not\simeq G_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≄</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\not\simeq G_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists at least one hidden graph <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{(p)}(G_{v},H_{j})\neq\mathcal{K}^{(p)}(G_{u},H_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{(p)}(G_{v},H_{j})\neq\mathcal{K}^{(p)}(G_{u},H_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(G_{v})\neq\Phi(G_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(G_{v})\neq\Phi(G_{u})</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, this implies <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}^{(t+1)}\neq c_{u}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}^{(t+1)}\neq c_{u}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the overall mapping <math id="A1.SSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\mapsto c_{v}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\mapsto c_{v}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective over the set of rooted subgraphs, consistent with the SWL refinement definition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Proof of Corollary 1</h3>

<div id="Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two graphs, and let <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> denote a fixed subgraph extraction policy. If the SWL test distinguishes <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under policy <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, then MoSE also distinguishes <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the same policy, provided the conditions in <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ Proof of Proposition 2 ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis ‣ MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> hold.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove the corollary by introducing the following definition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. 2023</a>)</cite> and lemma first.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be two color refinement algorithms, and denote <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> as the graph representation computed by <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for graph <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We say:</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is no less powerful than </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, if there exist a pair of non-isomorphic graphs </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(G)\neq c_{1}(G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(G)\neq c_{1}(G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}(G)=c_{2}(G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}(G)=c_{2}(G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is more powerful than </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, if for any pair of graphs </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, if </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}(G)\neq c_{2}(G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}(G)\neq c_{2}(G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(G)\neq c_{1}(G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(G)\neq c_{1}(G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the color of nodes in a graph cannot be further refined by <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> when initialized with the stable coloring from <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is more powerful than <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The above lemma allows us to assess the relative expressive power of two color refinement algorithms on a single graph instead of requiring a pair of graphs for comparison. We now prove that MoSE is more powerful than SWL.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a graph <math id="A1.SSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.SSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the stable color of node <math id="A1.SSx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> from MoSE. We take these stable colors as the initial colors of SWL, </span><span id="A1.SSx3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span><span id="A1.SSx3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">, <math id="A1.SSx3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}^{S,(0)}=c_{v}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}^{S,(0)}=c_{v}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the first SWL iteration, we have:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{v}^{S,(1)}=\operatorname{hash}(G_{v}^{S,(0)})=\operatorname{hash}\left(\left(\mathcal{V}_{v},\mathcal{E}_{v},c_{v}^{M}\right)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>hash</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>hash</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{v}^{S,(1)}=\operatorname{hash}(G_{v}^{S,(0)})=\operatorname{hash}\left(\left(\mathcal{V}_{v},\mathcal{E}_{v},c_{v}^{M}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider two arbitrary nodes <math id="A1.SSx3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,u</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="A1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{M}_{v}=c^{M}_{u}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{M}_{v}=c^{M}_{u}.</annotation></semantics></math> By <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ Proof of Proposition 2 ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis ‣ MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the rooted subgraphs <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are color-isomorphic under <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the injectivity of the hash yields <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{hash}(G^{S,(0)}_{v})=\operatorname{hash}(G^{S,(0)}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>hash</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>hash</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{hash}(G^{S,(0)}_{v})=\operatorname{hash}(G^{S,(0)}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{S,(1)}_{v}=c^{S,(1)}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{S,(1)}_{v}=c^{S,(1)}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, SWL cannot split a color class that MoSE has already formed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{M}_{v}\neq c^{M}_{u}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{M}_{v}\neq c^{M}_{u}.</annotation></semantics></math> Again by <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ Proof of Proposition 2 ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis ‣ MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> the subgraphs <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are not color-isomorphic, so <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{hash}(G^{(0)}_{v})\neq\operatorname{hash}(G^{(0)}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>hash</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>hash</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{hash}(G^{(0)}_{v})\neq\operatorname{hash}(G^{(0)}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{S,(1)}_{v}\neq c^{S,(1)}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{S,(1)}_{v}\neq c^{S,(1)}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, SWL never merges two distinct MoSE classes.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experimental Supplementary</h2>

<section id="A2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Training Procedure of MoSE </h3>

<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Training Procedure</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.3.1" class="ltx_listingline">


<span id="algorithm1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> Extracted Subgraphs <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}=(\mathcal{V}_{v},\mathcal{E}_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}=(\mathcal{V}_{v},\mathcal{E}_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>; Subgraph Experts <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E=\{E_{s}:s=1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\{E_{s}:s=1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{s}=\{H_{1}^{s},\ldots,H_{N}^{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{s}=\{H_{1}^{s},\ldots,H_{N}^{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with hidden graphs
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> Node Embeddings <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, Learned hidden graphs
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.3.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="epoch=1,...,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">epoch=1,...,M</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.3.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.3.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.3.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.3.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            Calculate the expert scores and select experts via Eqs.(7)-(9);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            Compute MoE loss <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{importance}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{importance}</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. (15);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.3.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1,...,P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1,...,P</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.3.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            Calculate the <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-step random walk kernel between <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in selected experts via Eq. (11);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            Concatenate the kernel values and map the results as <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}_{s}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}_{s}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. (10);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            Combine the node embedding with expert scores as <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. (12);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.11" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> </span>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">9</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.3.11.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">(Readout the graph embedding <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝒉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for graph-level task);</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.3.12.2" class="ltx_text"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            Compute the task loss <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{task}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{task}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            Final loss <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{task}+\beta\cdot\mathcal{L}_{importance}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{task}+\beta\cdot\mathcal{L}_{importance}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.15" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.3.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span>
            Back propagation, update parameters;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.3.16" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Dataset Details and Statistics</h3>

<div id="A2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We here introduce the details of the datasets used in our work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09337v1/GraphFive_example.svg" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/191;" width="698" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Illustration of the 5 types of graphs in synthetic dataset GraphFive</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Graph Classification Datasets.</h4>

<div id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We choose 10 datasets, including 8 real-world graphs and 2 synthetic graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For real-world graphs, MUTAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debnath et al. 1991</a>)</cite>, D&amp;D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dobson and Doig 2003</a>)</cite>, NCI1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wale, Watson, and Karypis 2008</a>)</cite>, PROTEINS, and ENZYMES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borgwardt et al. 2005</a>)</cite> are bio/chemo-informatics datasets. MUTAG consists of 188 mutagenic aromatic and heteroaromatic nitro compounds. The task is to predict whether or not each chemical compound has mutagenic effects on the Gram-negative bacterium Salmonella typhimurium. D&amp;D contains over a thousand protein structures. Each protein is a graph whose nodes correspond to amino acids. The task is to predict if a protein is an enzyme or not. NCI1 contains more than four thousand chemical compounds screened for activity against non-small cell lung cancer and ovarian cancer cell lines. PROTEINS consists of proteins represented as graphs where vertices are secondary structure elements, and there is an edge between two vertices if they are neighbors in the amino-acid sequence or in 3D space. The task is to classify proteins into enzymes and non-enzymes. ENZYMES contains 600 protein tertiary structures obtained from the BRENDA enzyme database. Each enzyme is a member of one of the Enzyme Commission top level enzyme classes. IMDB-BINARY, IMDB-MULTI, and REDDIT-BINARY <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yanardag and Vishwanathan 2015</a>)</cite> are social interaction datasets. IMDB-BINARY and IMDB-MULTI are created from IMDB, an online database of information related to movies and television programs. The graphs contained in the two datasets correspond to movie collaborations. The vertices of each graph represent actors/actresses and two vertices are connected by an edge if the corresponding actors/actresses appear in the same movie. The task is to predict which genre an ego-network belongs to. REDDIT-BINARY contains graphs that model the social interactions between users of Reddit. Each graph represents an online discussion thread. The task is to classify graphs into either communities or subreddits.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For synthetic datasets, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> to generate GraphFive and GraphCycle. For GraphFive, we first generate 8 15 Barabási-Albert graphs as communities, each containing 10 200 nodes. Then, we connect the generated BA graphs in pre-defined five shapes: caveman, cycle grid, ladder, and star. To connect nodes in different clusters, we randomly add edges with a probability ranging from 0.05 to 0.15. We give an illustration of the 5 types of graphs in <a href="#A2.F7" title="In Dataset Details and Statistics ‣ Appendix B Experimental Supplementary ‣ MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. For GraphCycle, we first generate 8-15 Barabási-Albert graphs as communities, each containing 10-200 nodes. Then, we connect the generated BA graphs in pre-defined two shapes: Cycle and Non-Cycle. To connect nodes in different clusters, we randomly add edges with a probability ranging from 0.05 to 0.15.
The statistics of these datasets are shown in <a href="#A2.T4" title="In Graph Classification Datasets. ‣ Dataset Details and Statistics ‣ Appendix B Experimental Supplementary ‣ MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
